the target language is: lps.

% The interest rate reset of the CDM Smart Contract Task Force, in Logical
% English for LPS.
%
% Source: the Common Domain Model (CDM) of FINOS, namespace
% cdm.event.instructioncomposition.reset (event-instructioncomposition-reset-
% func.rosetta and -type.rosetta), and its Java tests, which give the
% scenarios below their data and their expected answers. The CDM is published
% under the Community Specification License 1.0; see README.md and sources/.
%
% A reset observes a floating rate (a term rate, SOFR, a compounded index) and
% fixes the rate of the next payment. The CDM writes it as eight steps, each
% of which adds its output to a record of the reset, the "reset instruction
% state". Here each step is an action, the record is a set of fluents, and
% the timeline of a run is the history of the reset: which step ran when,
% and what it added. The CDM leaves the function that picks the next step
% (ResetInstructionNextStep) without a body; the rules "the next step of ..."
% below give it one: the next step is the first step whose output is missing.
% A step that needs outside data (the ISDA floating rate option matrix, the
% market rates) waits until the data has arrived.
%
% Dates are worked out by the companion file interest_rate_reset.lps: the day
% before or after a date, the days of a period, the number of days between
% two dates, and the month of a date.

the maximum time is 16.

the events are:
    *a trade* asks for a reset; known as reset_requested.
    the floating rate option matrix describes *an index* as *a category* of style *a style* computed by *a method*; known as fro_described.
    the rate source publishes *a rate* for *an index* on *a date*; known as rate_published.

the actions are:
    *a trade* performs *a step*; known as performs.

the fluents are:
    *a trade* is being reset; known as resetting.
    *an index* is listed as *a category* of style *a style* computed by *a method*; known as fro_listed.
    *a rate* is the rate for *an index* on *a date*; known as market_rate.
    *a trade* has collected the index *an index* traded on *a date*; known as collected_index.
    *a trade* has the unadjusted period from *a date* to *a second date* reset on *a third date*; known as unadjusted_period.
    *a trade* has the adjusted period from *a date* to *a second date*; known as adjusted_period.
    *a trade* has the adjusted reset date *a date*; known as adjusted_reset_date.
    *a trade* has *an observation type* observation from *a date* to *a second date*; known as unadjusted_observation.
    *a trade* observes the rates from *a date* to *a second date*; known as adjusted_observation.
    *a trade* has the reset value *a value* by *a method*; known as reset_value.
    *a trade* has output the reset *a value* on *a date*; known as reset_output.
    the next step of *a trade* is *a step*; known as next_step.
    the output of *a step* is missing for *a trade*; known as output_missing.
    *a step* is ready for *a trade*; known as step_ready.
    the observation type of *a trade* is *an observation type*; known as observation_type.
    *a trade* calls for several observations; known as calls_for_several.
    *a trade* calls for a compounded index; known as calls_for_index.
    the compounding factor of *a trade* up to *a day* is *a number*; known as compounding_factor.
    *a trade* has a rate for each day observed; known as rates_complete.

the templates are:
    *a step* is step *a number*.
    the floating rate index of *a trade* is *an index*.
    the trade date of *a trade* is *a date*.
    the calculation period of *a trade* runs from *a date* to *a second date*.
    the reset date of *a trade* is *a date*.
    the business day convention of *a trade* is *a convention*.
    the fixing offset of *a trade* is *a number* business days.
    the lookback of *a trade* is *a number* business days.
    the method in the confirmation of *a trade* is *a method*.
    the day count of *a trade* is *a fraction*.
    the basis of *a fraction* is *a number*.
    *a date* is a holiday for *a trade*.
    *a date* is a business day for *a trade*.
    the preceding business day for *a trade* of *a date* is *a second date*.
    the following business day for *a trade* of *a date* is *a second date*.
    *a date* adjusted for *a trade* by *a convention* is *a second date*.
    *a date* shifted for *a trade* by *a number* business days is *a second date*.
    *a date* is the day before *a second date*; known as day_before.
    *a date* is the day after *a second date*; known as day_after.
    *a day* is a calendar day from *a date* to *a second date*; known as calendar_day.
    *a number* is the number of days from *a date* to *a second date*; known as days_between.
    the month of *a date* is *a month*; known as month_of.
    *a date* is later than *a second date*; known as date_later.

the knowledge base interest rate reset includes:

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% The eight steps, in the CDM's order (ResetInstructionStepsEnum).

CollectFloatingRateOption is step 1.
DetermineUnadjustedCalculationPeriod is step 2.
AdjustPeriod is step 3.
AdjustDate is step 4.
DetermineUnadjustedObservationDates is step 5.
AdjustObservationDates is step 6.
CalculateResetValue is step 7.
ResetInstructionOutput is step 8.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Running the reset. A request opens the record; the next step runs as soon
% as it is ready; the last step closes the record.

when a trade asks for a reset
then the trade is being reset.

when the floating rate option matrix describes an index as a category of style a style computed by a method
then the index is listed as the category of style the style computed by the method.

when the rate source publishes a rate for an index on a date
then the rate is the rate for the index on the date.

if the next step of a trade is a step at a first time
    and the step is ready for the trade at the first time
then the trade performs the step from a second time to a third time.

% (The step has times of its own, so that a step the CDM refuses waits, and
% is refused again at each cycle, rather than ending the run: le-for-lps.md
% §3.5.)

% The next step is the first step whose output is missing
% (ResetInstructionNextStep, whose body the CDM leaves empty).

the next step of a trade is a step at a time if
    the trade is being reset at the time
    and the step is step a number N
    and the output of the step is missing for the trade at the time
    and it is not the case that
        a second step is step a number M
        and M < N
        and the output of the second step is missing for the trade at the time.

the output of CollectFloatingRateOption is missing for a trade at a time if
    the trade is being reset at the time
    and it is not the case that the trade has collected the index an index traded on a date at the time.

the output of DetermineUnadjustedCalculationPeriod is missing for a trade at a time if
    the trade is being reset at the time
    and it is not the case that the trade has the unadjusted period from a date to a second date reset on a third date at the time.

the output of AdjustPeriod is missing for a trade at a time if
    the trade is being reset at the time
    and it is not the case that the trade has the adjusted period from a date to a second date at the time.

the output of AdjustDate is missing for a trade at a time if
    the trade is being reset at the time
    and it is not the case that the trade has the adjusted reset date a date at the time.

the output of DetermineUnadjustedObservationDates is missing for a trade at a time if
    the trade is being reset at the time
    and it is not the case that the trade has an observation type observation from a date to a second date at the time.

the output of AdjustObservationDates is missing for a trade at a time if
    the trade is being reset at the time
    and it is not the case that the trade observes the rates from a date to a second date at the time.

the output of CalculateResetValue is missing for a trade at a time if
    the trade is being reset at the time
    and it is not the case that the trade has the reset value a value by a method at the time.

the output of ResetInstructionOutput is missing for a trade at a time if
    the trade is being reset at the time
    and it is not the case that the trade has output the reset a value on a date at the time.

% A step that works only on the trade's own terms is always ready. Step 5
% needs the index's entry in the ISDA floating rate option matrix, and step 7
% needs a market rate for every day observed.

CollectFloatingRateOption is ready for a trade at a time if
    the trade is being reset at the time.

DetermineUnadjustedCalculationPeriod is ready for a trade at a time if
    the trade is being reset at the time.

AdjustPeriod is ready for a trade at a time if
    the trade is being reset at the time.

AdjustDate is ready for a trade at a time if
    the trade is being reset at the time.

DetermineUnadjustedObservationDates is ready for a trade at a time if
    the trade has collected the index an index traded on a date at the time
    and the index is listed as a category of style a style computed by a method at the time.

AdjustObservationDates is ready for a trade at a time if
    the trade is being reset at the time.

CalculateResetValue is ready for a trade at a time if
    the trade has a rate for each day observed at the time.

ResetInstructionOutput is ready for a trade at a time if
    the trade is being reset at the time.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Step 1. Collect the floating rate option: the index and the trade date,
% from the confirmation.

when a trade performs CollectFloatingRateOption
    and the floating rate index of the trade is an index
    and the trade date of the trade is a date
then the trade has collected the index the index traded on the date.

% Step 2. Determine the unadjusted calculation period, and the reset date.

when a trade performs DetermineUnadjustedCalculationPeriod
    and the calculation period of the trade runs from a first date to a second date
    and the reset date of the trade is a third date
then the trade has the unadjusted period from the first date to the second date reset on the third date.

% Step 3. Adjust the calculation period to the business day convention.

when a trade performs AdjustPeriod
    and the trade has the unadjusted period from a first date to a second date reset on a third date
    and the business day convention of the trade is a convention
    and the first date adjusted for the trade by the convention is a fourth date
    and the second date adjusted for the trade by the convention is a fifth date
then the trade has the adjusted period from the fourth date to the fifth date.

% Step 4. Adjust the reset date.

when a trade performs AdjustDate
    and the trade has the unadjusted period from a first date to a second date reset on a third date
    and the business day convention of the trade is a convention
    and the third date adjusted for the trade by the convention is a fourth date
then the trade has the adjusted reset date the fourth date.

% Step 5. Determine the unadjusted observation dates: the reset date alone
% for a single observation, every calendar day of the period for several
% observations, and the first and last days of the period for a compounded
% index.

when a trade performs DetermineUnadjustedObservationDates
    and the observation type of the trade is Single
    and the trade has the adjusted reset date a date
then the trade has Single observation from the date to the date.

when a trade performs DetermineUnadjustedObservationDates
    and the observation type of the trade is Multiple
    and the trade has the adjusted period from a first date to a second date
then the trade has Multiple observation from the first date to the second date.

when a trade performs DetermineUnadjustedObservationDates
    and the observation type of the trade is CompoundedIndex
    and the trade has the adjusted period from a first date to a second date
then the trade has CompoundedIndex observation from the first date to the second date.

% An overnight index needs the method of calculation from the confirmation
% (the CDM's condition MethodFromConfirmationExistsWhenOvernightRate).

it must not be true that
    a trade performs DetermineUnadjustedObservationDates
    and the trade has collected the index an index traded on a date
    and the index is listed as a category of style Overnight computed by a method
    and the method in the confirmation of the trade is none.

% The observation type (DetermineObservationType).

the observation type of a trade is Single at a time if
    the trade has collected the index an index traded on a date at the time
    and the index is listed as ScreenRate of style a style computed by none at the time
    and the style is different from Overnight
    and the method in the confirmation of the trade is none.

the observation type of a trade is Multiple at a time if
    the trade calls for several observations at the time
    and it is not the case that
        the observation type of the trade is Single at the time.

the observation type of a trade is CompoundedIndex at a time if
    the trade calls for a compounded index at the time
    and it is not the case that
        the trade calls for several observations at the time.

a trade calls for several observations at a time if
    the trade has collected the index an index traded on a date at the time
    and the index is listed as Calculated of style CompoundedFRO computed by a method at the time
    and the method is OISCompound
    and the method in the confirmation of the trade is none.

a trade calls for several observations at a time if
    the trade has collected the index an index traded on a date at the time
    and the index is listed as Calculated of style CompoundedFRO computed by a method at the time
    and the method in the confirmation of the trade is Compounding.

a trade calls for several observations at a time if
    the trade has collected the index an index traded on a date at the time
    and the index is listed as ScreenRate of style Overnight computed by a method at the time
    and the method in the confirmation of the trade is Compounding.

a trade calls for a compounded index at a time if
    the trade has collected the index an index traded on a date at the time
    and the index is listed as Calculated of style CompoundedIndex computed by Compounded at the time
    and the method in the confirmation of the trade is none.

a trade calls for a compounded index at a time if
    the trade has collected the index an index traded on a date at the time
    and the index is listed as Calculated of style CompoundedIndex computed by a method at the time
    and the method in the confirmation of the trade is CompoundedIndex.

% Step 6. Adjust the observation dates. A single observation moves by the
% fixing offset; a compounded index keeps its two dates; several
% observations move by the fixing offset and then back by the lookback, and
% each calendar day is then observed on the business day on or before it.

when a trade performs AdjustObservationDates
    and the trade has Single observation from a first date to the first date
    and the fixing offset of the trade is a number N business days
    and the first date shifted for the trade by N business days is a second date
then the trade observes the rates from the second date to the second date.

when a trade performs AdjustObservationDates
    and the trade has CompoundedIndex observation from a first date to a second date
then the trade observes the rates from the first date to the second date.

when a trade performs AdjustObservationDates
    and the trade has Multiple observation from a first date to a second date
    and the fixing offset of the trade is a number N business days
    and the lookback of the trade is a number L business days
    and a number K = 0 - L
    and the first date shifted for the trade by N business days is a third date
    and the third date shifted for the trade by K business days is a fourth date
    and the second date shifted for the trade by N business days is a fifth date
    and the fifth date shifted for the trade by K business days is a sixth date
    and the preceding business day for the trade of the fourth date is a seventh date
    and the preceding business day for the trade of the sixth date is an eighth date
then the trade observes the rates from the seventh date to the eighth date.

a trade has a rate for each day observed at a time if
    the trade has Single observation from a start date to an end date at the time
    and the trade has collected the index an index traded on a date at the time
    and the trade observes the rates from a first date to the first date at the time
    and a rate is the rate for the index on the first date at the time.

a trade has a rate for each day observed at a time if
    the trade has CompoundedIndex observation from a start date to an end date at the time
    and the trade has collected the index an index traded on a date at the time
    and the trade observes the rates from a first date to a second date at the time
    and a rate is the rate for the index on the first date at the time
    and a second rate is the rate for the index on the second date at the time.

a trade has a rate for each day observed at a time if
    the trade has Multiple observation from a start date to an end date at the time
    and the trade has collected the index an index traded on a date at the time
    and the trade observes the rates from a first date to a second date at the time
    and a number D is the count of each day such that
        the day is a calendar day from the first date to the second date
    and a number R is the count of each day such that
        the day is a calendar day from the first date to the second date
        and the preceding business day for the trade of the day is a third date
        and a rate is the rate for the index on the third date at the time
    and D = R.

% Step 7. Calculate the reset value.
% A single observation is the rate itself, unrounded.

when a trade performs CalculateResetValue
    and the trade has Single observation from a first date to a second date
    and the trade has collected the index an index traded on a date
    and the trade observes the rates from a third date to the third date
    and a rate is the rate for the index on the third date
then the trade has the reset value the rate by none.

% A compounded index: (the index at the end / the index at the start - 1)
% times the day count basis, over the days of the period, rounded to seven
% decimals.

when a trade performs CalculateResetValue
    and the trade has CompoundedIndex observation from a first date to a second date
    and the trade has collected the index an index traded on a date
    and the trade observes the rates from a third date to a fourth date
    and a number S is the rate for the index on the third date
    and a number E is the rate for the index on the fourth date
    and the trade has the adjusted period from a fifth date to a sixth date
    and a number G is the number of days from the fifth date to the sixth date
    and the day count of the trade is a fraction
    and the basis of the fraction is a number B
    and a number X = (E / S - 1) * B / (G + 1)
    and a number V = round(X * 10000000) / 10000000
then the trade has the reset value V by CompoundedIndex.

% Several observations, compounded: the product, over the days observed, of
% (1 + the rate / the basis), less 1, times the basis, over the number of
% days, rounded to seven decimals.

when a trade performs CalculateResetValue
    and the trade has Multiple observation from a first date to a second date
    and the trade observes the rates from a third date to a fourth date
    and the compounding factor of the trade up to the fourth date is a number F
    and a number G is the number of days from the third date to the fourth date
    and the day count of the trade is a fraction
    and the basis of the fraction is a number B
    and a number X = (F - 1) * B / (G + 1)
    and a number V = round(X * 10000000) / 10000000
then the trade has the reset value V by Compounding.

the compounding factor of a trade up to a day is a number F at a time if
    the trade observes the rates from the day to a second date at the time
    and the trade has collected the index an index traded on a date at the time
    and the preceding business day for the trade of the day is a third date
    and a number R is the rate for the index on the third date at the time
    and the day count of the trade is a fraction
    and the basis of the fraction is a number B
    and F = 1 + R / B.

the compounding factor of a trade up to a day is a number F at a time if
    the trade observes the rates from a first date to a second date at the time
    and the day is later than the first date
    and a third date is the day before the day
    and the compounding factor of the trade up to the third date is a number P at the time
    and the trade has collected the index an index traded on a date at the time
    and the preceding business day for the trade of the day is a fourth date
    and a number R is the rate for the index on the fourth date at the time
    and the day count of the trade is a fraction
    and the basis of the fraction is a number B
    and F = P * (1 + R / B).

% Step 8. Record the reset: its value, on the adjusted reset date.

when a trade performs ResetInstructionOutput
    and the trade has the reset value a value by a method
    and the trade has the adjusted reset date a date
then the trade has output the reset the value on the date.

when a trade performs ResetInstructionOutput
then it is not the case that the trade is being reset.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Business days. A date is a business day unless it is a holiday of the
% trade's calendar (the CDM's GetNonBusinessDates); weekends are holidays
% only where the calendar lists them, as in the CDM's tests.

a date is a business day for a trade if
    it is not the case that
        the date is a holiday for the trade.

the preceding business day for a trade of a date is the date if
    the date is a business day for the trade.

the preceding business day for a trade of a date is a second date if
    the date is a holiday for the trade
    and a third date is the day before the date
    and the preceding business day for the trade of the third date is the second date.

the following business day for a trade of a date is the date if
    the date is a business day for the trade.

the following business day for a trade of a date is a second date if
    the date is a holiday for the trade
    and a third date is the day after the date
    and the following business day for the trade of the third date is the second date.

a date adjusted for a trade by Following is a second date if
    the following business day for the trade of the date is the second date.

a date adjusted for a trade by Preceding is a second date if
    the preceding business day for the trade of the date is the second date.

a date adjusted for a trade by ModFollowing is a second date if
    the following business day for the trade of the date is the second date
    and the month of the date is a month
    and the month of the second date is the month.

a date adjusted for a trade by ModFollowing is a second date if
    the following business day for the trade of the date is a third date
    and the month of the date is a month
    and the month of the third date is a second month
    and the second month is different from the month
    and the preceding business day for the trade of the date is the second date.

a date adjusted for a trade by NotAdjusted is the date.

% Moving a date by a number of business days (ShiftBusinessDays): step one
% calendar day at a time, and count only the business days.

a date shifted for a trade by 0 business days is the date.

a date shifted for a trade by a number N business days is a second date if
    N < 0
    and a third date is the day before the date
    and the third date is a holiday for the trade
    and the third date shifted for the trade by N business days is the second date.

a date shifted for a trade by a number N business days is a second date if
    N < 0
    and a third date is the day before the date
    and the third date is a business day for the trade
    and a number M = N + 1
    and the third date shifted for the trade by M business days is the second date.

a date shifted for a trade by a number N business days is a second date if
    N > 0
    and a third date is the day after the date
    and the third date is a holiday for the trade
    and the third date shifted for the trade by N business days is the second date.

a date shifted for a trade by a number N business days is a second date if
    N > 0
    and a third date is the day after the date
    and the third date is a business day for the trade
    and a number M = N - 1
    and the third date shifted for the trade by M business days is the second date.

the basis of ACT_360 is 360.
the basis of ACT_365_FIXED is 365.
the basis of CAL_252 is 252.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% The trades. Each takes its data from a test of the CDM.

% A term rate, observed once, two business days before the reset date; the
% 8th of January is a holiday, so the rate is observed on the 7th
% (Create_AdjustObservationDatesInstructionTest, "Skips non-business dates
% when applying fixing day offset"; the rate passes through unrounded, as in
% Create_CalculateResetValueInstructionTest).

the floating rate index of term1 is USD_TERM.
the trade date of term1 is 2023-12-01.
the calculation period of term1 runs from 2024-01-10 to 2024-04-09.
the reset date of term1 is 2024-01-10.
the business day convention of term1 is Following.
the fixing offset of term1 is -2 business days.
the lookback of term1 is 0 business days.
the method in the confirmation of term1 is none.
the day count of term1 is ACT_360.
2024-01-08 is a holiday for term1.

% SOFR compounded in arrears over three days, at 5%, 6% and 5.5%
% (Create_CalculateResetValueInstructionTest, "Multiple observation handles
% varying rates").

the floating rate index of sofr1 is USD_SOFR_COMPOUND.
the trade date of sofr1 is 2023-12-01.
the calculation period of sofr1 runs from 2024-01-01 to 2024-01-03.
the reset date of sofr1 is 2024-01-03.
the business day convention of sofr1 is ModFollowing.
the fixing offset of sofr1 is 0 business days.
the lookback of sofr1 is 0 business days.
the method in the confirmation of sofr1 is none.
the day count of sofr1 is ACT_360.

% SOFR over a weekend: Saturday 6 and Sunday 7 January are holidays, so they
% are observed at Friday's rate (Create_AdjustObservationDatesInstructionTest,
% "Collapses non-business dates to preceding business day and fills missing
% calendar days", with the rates of "mixed: repeated + distinct dates,
% varying rates").

the floating rate index of sofr2 is USD_SOFR_COMPOUND.
the trade date of sofr2 is 2023-12-01.
the calculation period of sofr2 runs from 2024-01-05 to 2024-01-08.
the reset date of sofr2 is 2024-01-08.
the business day convention of sofr2 is ModFollowing.
the fixing offset of sofr2 is 0 business days.
the lookback of sofr2 is 0 business days.
the method in the confirmation of sofr2 is Compounding.
the day count of sofr2 is ACT_360.
2024-01-06 is a holiday for sofr2.
2024-01-07 is a holiday for sofr2.

% SOFR with a lookback of two business days: the period from the 8th to the
% 12th of January is observed from the 6th to the 10th
% (Create_AdjustObservationDatesInstructionTest, "Applies lookback shift").

the floating rate index of sofr3 is USD_SOFR_COMPOUND.
the trade date of sofr3 is 2023-12-01.
the calculation period of sofr3 runs from 2024-01-08 to 2024-01-12.
the reset date of sofr3 is 2024-01-12.
the business day convention of sofr3 is ModFollowing.
the fixing offset of sofr3 is 0 business days.
the lookback of sofr3 is 2 business days.
the method in the confirmation of sofr3 is none.
the day count of sofr3 is ACT_360.

% A compounded index, from 1000 to 1005 over January
% (Create_CalculateResetValueInstructionTest, "Compounded Index").

the floating rate index of index1 is USD_SOFR_INDEX.
the trade date of index1 is 2023-12-01.
the calculation period of index1 runs from 2024-01-01 to 2024-01-31.
the reset date of index1 is 2024-01-31.
the business day convention of index1 is Following.
the fixing offset of index1 is 0 business days.
the lookback of index1 is 0 business days.
the method in the confirmation of index1 is none.
the day count of index1 is ACT_360.

% A period ending on a holiday at the end of a month: Modified Following goes
% back to the 30th of March rather than into April
% (Create_AdjustDateInstructionTest, "MODFOLLOWING: falls back to preceding").

the floating rate index of term2 is USD_TERM.
the trade date of term2 is 2024-02-01.
the calculation period of term2 runs from 2024-03-01 to 2024-03-31.
the reset date of term2 is 2024-03-31.
the business day convention of term2 is ModFollowing.
the fixing offset of term2 is 0 business days.
the lookback of term2 is 0 business days.
the method in the confirmation of term2 is none.
the day count of term2 is ACT_360.
2024-03-31 is a holiday for term2.

% An overnight index whose confirmation names no method: the CDM refuses
% step 5 (MethodFromConfirmationExistsWhenOvernightRate). Here the step is
% refused at every cycle, and the reset waits at it.

the floating rate index of overnight1 is USD_SOFR.
the trade date of overnight1 is 2023-12-01.
the calculation period of overnight1 runs from 2024-01-15 to 2024-01-17.
the reset date of overnight1 is 2024-01-17.
the business day convention of overnight1 is Following.
the fixing offset of overnight1 is 0 business days.
the lookback of overnight1 is 0 business days.
the method in the confirmation of overnight1 is none.
